{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"curl -LfsS https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pollar-xyz/pollar/main/UPGRADE.md | rg -n -C 3 '0.12|quoteRamp|baseUrl|sponsored|balance' | head -130\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"curl -LfsS https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pollar-xyz/pollar/main/UPGRADE.md | rg -n -C 3 '0.12|quoteRamp|baseUrl|sponsored|balance' | head -130\"","aggregated_output":"1-# Upgrade guide\n2-\n3:## 0.11.5 -> 0.12.0\n4-\n5-Use `@pollar/core`, `@pollar/react` and `@pollar/react-native` at matching\n6:versions: `@pollar/react@0.12.0` and `@pollar/react-native@0.12.0` require\n7:`@pollar/core@^0.12.0`. A caret range on 0.11.x (`^0.11.5`) does not reach\n8:0.12, so change it by hand.\n9-\n10-Upgrade `@pollar/stellar-wallets-kit-adapter` and\n11:`@pollar/solana-wallet-standard-adapter` to `0.12.0` too: their core range is\n12:`^0.11.2 || ^0.12.0`, while the releases before it (`^0.11.2`) refuse core 0.12.\n13:`@pollar/privy-adapter` and `@pollar/accesly-adapter` have no 0.12 release;\n14:their published ranges do not accept core 0.12.\n15-\n16-### API v3\n17-\n18:Every request goes to `/v3` of sdk-api. `baseUrl` stays an origin\n19:(`https://sdk.api.pollar.xyz`); the SDK appends the version, so a `baseUrl`\n20-that carried `/v2` must drop it. `/v3` serves the endpoints 0.11.x used on\n21-`/v2` with the same handlers, ramps excepted: Pollar v3 redefines the ramps\n22-API, and the ramps entries of this release describe what changed there. A\n--\n40-the `RampWorkflow*` types. A ramp is now an order of one or more legs (the\n41-provider, a swap, a bridge) that Pollar runs server-side:\n42-\n43:| 0.11.x                                       | 0.12.0                                                                               |\n44-| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n45-| `getRampRoutes()`                            | `getRampRoutes(direction)`: fiat and asset pairs, with the legs of each              |\n46-| `getRampCountries()`                         | `rampCountries(routes)`                                                              |\n47:| `getRampsQuote(query)`                       | `quoteRamp({ direction, fiat, asset, amount: { value, denomination } })`, best first |\n48-| quote `requiredFields`                       | `rampQuoteFields(quote)`; send the answers as `payee`                                |\n49-| `createOnRamp` / `createOffRamp`             | `createRampOrder({ quoteId, payee })`                                                |\n50-| `getRampTransaction` / `pollRampTransaction` | `getRampOrder(id)` / `pollRampOrder(id, { onUpdate, signal })`                       |\n--\n388-| `WalletBalanceModalTemplateProps` | `chains`, `onSelectChain` optional (pass both to show the picker); new optional `assetMetadata`                                           |\n389-| `KycModalTemplateProps`           | new optional `reviewReason`, `processing`, `error`, `onStartAgain`                                                                        |\n390-\n391:The built-in Send, Receive and Wallet balance modals keep the network picker:\n392-it renders when the app has two or more chains, so a single-chain app shows\n393-none. A template you mount yourself shows it only when you pass both `chains`\n394-and `onSelectChain`.\n--\n517-`chains` envelope and an unreadable chain is now represented explicitly instead\n518-of being flattened away, which changes two public types.\n519-\n520:### 1. `balance` and `available` are now `string | null`\n521-\n522:`WalletBalanceRecord.balance` and `.available` were `string`. They are now\n523-nullable: `null` means **the chain could not be read** (an unreachable RPC), not\n524-an empty wallet. It is never coerced to `'0'`, precisely so the UI can tell the\n525-two apart.\n526-\n527-```ts\n528-// BEFORE (0.11.0) — always a string\n529:const n = parseFloat(record.balance);\n530-\n531-// AFTER (0.11.1) — null is \"unavailable\", not zero\n532:const n = record.balance === null ? null : parseFloat(record.balance);\n533-```\n534-\n535-Render `null` as a dash or an \"unavailable\" state. Rendering it as `0` tells the\n--\n542-handles Stellar, Polygon and Solana alike. If you special-cased \"Solana only has\n543-SOL\", drop that branch.\n544-\n545:### 3. New balance fields\n546-\n547:`decimals?: number`, `limit?: string` and `sponsored?: boolean` join the record,\n548-and `type` gained `'token'` alongside the Stellar classic values. Format amounts\n549-against each token's own `decimals` (Stellar stays at 7) rather than assuming 7\n550-everywhere. An exhaustive `switch` on `type` needs a `'token'` arm.\n--\n582-The wrapper components (`<WalletBalanceModal>` and friends) do this for you — no\n583-change needed if you use those.\n584-\n585:### 5. `setTrustline` drops the `sponsored` opt-in flag\n586-\n587:`setTrustline(asset, { sponsored: true })` no longer type-checks. Who pays is now\n588-decided server-side from the app config, not by a caller flag: embedded wallets\n589-hit `POST /wallet/assets/trustline` (the server sponsors or self-pays, then\n590-submits) and external wallets co-sign whichever XDR the build endpoint returns.\n--\n593-\n594-```ts\n595-// BEFORE (0.11.0)\n596:await setTrustline(asset, { sponsored: true });\n597-\n598-// AFTER (0.11.1) — drop the flag; the app config decides\n599-await setTrustline(asset);\n--\n628-\n629-### 1. The SDK now calls `/v2`\n630-\n631:`basePath` is built as `${baseUrl}/v2`. If you pass a custom `baseUrl`, keep\n632-passing the **origin only** (`https://sdk.api.example.com`) — the SDK appends the\n633-version prefix itself. Your backend must serve the `/v2` routes; an sdk-api that\n634-only has `/v1` will 404 across the board.\n--\n636-### 2. Balances are multichain\n637-\n638-`WalletBalanceRecord` gained `chain` (`'STELLAR' | 'POLYGON' | 'SOLANA'`), and\n639:the balance response gained `multichain`, set when more than one chain came back.\n640-Records minted before multichain carry no `chain`; treat absent as `'STELLAR'`\n641-rather than as unknown.\n642-\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
